# A name that does not come back, and what it answers.
#
# `exit!` leaves, so what it answers is `Never`: the type with no
# constructors, which nothing holds because there is nothing to hold. That
# is not written anywhere here. `leave!` ends in `exit!`, so `leave!`
# answers `Never` too, and `main!` after it, each worked out rather than
# declared.
#
# What it buys is what is not here. Nothing is laid out after the call to
# `leave!`, on any target, because nothing comes back to it -- and a clause
# that leaves is not measured against the clauses that do not, so a name
# with one arm that exits still answers whatever its other arm answers.

type Nat Zero Succ(Nat)

add(a Zero) -> a
add(a Succ(k)) -> Succ(add(a k))

leave!(h n) -> exit!(h n)

main!(h) ->
  two = Succ(Succ(Zero))
  leave!(h add(two two))
